<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amanullah Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amanullah Khan ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 1919 to 1929, first as emir and from 1926 as king. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-afghan-war">Third Anglo-Afghan War</a> in 1919 and won full control of foreign policy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. His rapid modernising reforms, including dress codes and education for women, provoked a rebellion that forced him to abdicate in 1929, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan.jpg" alt="Amanullah Khan" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Amanullah_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amanullah Khan was born on 1 June 1892 at Paghman near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, the third son of Emir Habibullah Khan by his second wife. He became governor of Kabul and held command of the army and the treasury. On 20 February 1919 Habibullah, on a hunting trip in Laghman Province, was shot in the head in his tent by an unknown assassin.</p><p>Habibullah's brother Nasrullah Khan was declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> at Jalalabad the next day. Amanullah, who had stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, seized the treasury and the central government. He proclaimed himself Emir within days and, despite an oath on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> that Nasrullah would come to no harm, had him arrested on 3 March. A court held on 13 April executed a colonel for the murder and, on manufactured evidence, judged Nasrullah complicit. Nasrullah received a life sentence and was killed in jail about a year later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 3 May 1919 Amanullah launched a surprise attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-afghan-war">Third Anglo-Afghan War</a>. After early successes the fighting settled into a stalemate, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was still bearing the costs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. An armistice at the end of 1919 freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> of British control over its foreign affairs.</p><p>With his father-in-law and foreign minister Mahmud Tarzi he drafted the country's first constitution, ratified on 11 April 1922 by 872 tribal elders and officials at a Loya Jirga in Jalalabad. More than 320 schools were founded, primary education was made compulsory, and strict dress codes for women were overturned, a policy in which his wife Queen Soraya Tarzi took part. A rebellion at Khost against these changes was put down in 1925. From late 1927 to 1928 he toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and other countries, where he met rulers including King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his return, measures such as abolishing the veil, moving the weekly day of rest from Friday to Thursday and ordering Western clothes stirred conservative opposition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habibullah-kalakani">Habibullah Kalakani</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> on 14 December 1928 and took it on 7 January 1929. Amanullah abdicated on 14 January in favour of his half-brother Inayatullah Khan, who surrendered four days later. An attempt to retake the capital failed in April, and on 23 May 1929 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, never to return.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-nadir-shah">Mohammad Nadir Shah</a> replaced Kalakani on 13 October 1929 and reversed most of the reforms, though his successor King Zahir later introduced a more gradual programme. Amanullah settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where loyalists several times tried unsuccessfully to restore him. He died in Zürich on 26 April 1960 and was buried at Jalalabad near his father's tomb.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amanullah Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Amanullah Khan</b> built palaces and gardens in Sanandaj.</blockquote><a href="/state/ardalan">Ardalan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1919 <b>Amanullah Khan</b> fought the Third Anglo-Afghan War and won full independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Amanullah Khan</b>, who had won full independence from Britain in 1919, took the title king (padishah) in 1926, turning the Emirate of Afghanistan into a kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-afghanistan">Kingdom of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Amanullah Khan's</b> modernising reforms provoked tribal and religious revolts in late 1928.</blockquote><a href="/state/saqqawist-emirate">Saqqawist Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Paghman, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in Zurich, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.58,68.95&amp;year=1936" aria-label="Paghman on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6302 912 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amanullah Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6752 1182h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6752 1182h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.58,68.95&amp;year=1936">What was here in 1936? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
